-- title:
--   Rational Tate module of J₀(N) free of rank two
-- statement:
--   Let $N \ge 1$ and let $p$ be a prime. Assume [`ModularCurve.HeckeInputsAll N`](def/ModularCurve_HeckeInputsAll.html#L8): for every prime $\ell$ the predicate `HeckeInputsAlong` holds over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ for the level $N$ and $\ell$, i.e. the integrality conditions on the two Hecke correspondences, the principal-divisor hypothesis at level $N\ell$, finiteness of the $\alpha$-map, and the fundamental identity and norm formula. Assume also [`ModularCurve.HeckeOperatorsCommuteBar N`](def/ModularCurve_HeckeModule.html#L25), that the endomorphisms $\overline{T_\ell}$ of $J_0(N) = \mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the modular function field of level $N$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ commute pairwise. The module structure used is `heckeModuleBar N`, which under this commutativity is the action of $\mathbb T = \mathbb Z[X_\ell : \ell \text{ prime}]$ sending $X_\ell$ to $\overline{T_\ell}$. The assertion is that there exist a commutative $\mathbb Q_p$-algebra $T$, a ring homomorphism $\varphi \colon \mathbb T \to T$ whose image generates $T$ as a $\mathbb Q_p$-algebra, and a $\mathbb Q_p$-linear isomorphism $e$ from $\mathbb Q_p \otimes_{\mathbb Z_p} T_p J_0(N)$ (the Tate module being the group of sequences $(x_n)$ with $p^n x_n = 0$ and $p x_{n+1} = x_n$) onto $T \oplus T$, with $e(t \cdot v) = \varphi(t)\, e(v)$ componentwise for all $t \in \mathbb T$ and all $v$.
--
--   This is the rational Eichler–Shimura freeness statement: the $p$-adic rational Tate module of $J_0(N)$ is free of rank two over the $\mathbb Q_p$-algebra generated by the Hecke operators (Darmon–Diamond–Taylor, Lemma 1.39). It feeds the analysis of inertia-fixed vectors in the Tate module, the comparison of Hecke torsion with the square of a Hecke-lattice index, and the construction of rational rank-two cyclotomic families.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_exists_heckeEquivariant_linearEquiv_rationalTateModule_jZero_fun_two.lean

